---
// The privacy page: what the site keeps, in plain English, accurate to the
// code (ARCHITECTURE.md §8, §19). A first draft for the owner to review; not
// legal advice. Keep it in step with the code: a change to what's stored,
// logged or measured changes this page too.
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { CORRECTIONS_EMAIL } from "../config";
import {
  DAILY_LEADERBOARD_PATH,
  FRIENDLY_PATH,
  ENDLESS_LEADERBOARD_PATH,
  TWITCH_PATH,
} from "../lib/paths";

const TURNSTILE_ADDENDUM = "https://www.cloudflare.com/en-gb/turnstile-privacy-policy/";
---

<Page
  title="Privacy: No Cookies, No Accounts, No Ads | Bigger Than Game"
  description="What Bigger Than Game keeps and why: no cookies, no accounts and no ads. Your best score stays in your browser and game stats are anonymous. In plain English."
  stickyBar
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p>
    Bigger Than Game is built to know as little about you as possible. This page sets out everything
    the site keeps, where it's kept and for how long.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No cookies, no accounts and no sign-up.</li>
    <li>No advertising, and nothing that tracks you from site to site.</li>
    <li>Your best score is kept in your own browser, not by us.</li>
    <li>
      Putting a run on the Endless leaderboard is up to you. If you do, we keep the nickname you
      choose and the score for 100 days, and, if you choose, your country's flag; nothing else about
      you.
    </li>
    <li>
      Daily Ranked is a leaderboard game: the name you play under and your score go on that day's
      board, and are kept for 100 days, with your country's flag if you choose.
    </li>
    <li>Game statistics are anonymous: the only thing about you they record is your country.</li>
    <li>
      In Twitch Mode your browser reads your Twitch chat directly. Nothing your chat says, and no
      viewer's name, reaches us: only how many voted and which way.
    </li>
    <li>
      Feedback you send is emailed to us and also kept in our server logs for three days. Please
      don't include personal details in it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In your browser</h2>
  <p>
    Your best score is saved in your browser's local storage, one number per game mode, so the game
    can show it next time and tell you when you've beaten it. It never leaves your device, and
    clearing this site's data in your browser removes it. We don't set any cookies.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Endless, your browser also keeps your 10 best runs, with their dates and scores, so the{" "}
    <a href={ENDLESS_LEADERBOARD_PATH}>leaderboard page</a> can show them to you; the nickname you
    last published under, so it's filled in for you next time; and, the first time you publish a
    run, a random number made for this browser. Only that random number is ever sent, and only when
    you publish. Clearing this site's data removes all of it, and the next run you publish gets a
    new number.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Daily Ranked, your browser keeps the same random number, which is sent when you start the
    day's game and when the page checks whether you've played it; the name you last played under and
    your flag choice; and the day's game number with a random code for your run, so the game can
    pick up where you left off if the page is reloaded.
  </p>

  <h2>When you play</h2>
  <p>
    The game asks our server for each question as you go, so that the hidden number isn't sent to
    your browser before you've answered. Like every website, the server sees your IP address when
    your browser connects. We use it for limiting how fast a single connection can play, to stop
    anyone copying the game's data; the limit forgets it within a minute. The address itself never
    goes into our logs or statistics. The one other use is the scrambled, 48-hour count in Daily
    Ranked, described below.
  </p>
  <p>
    To see how the game plays and keep it fair, the server records anonymous game events: when a run
    starts and ends, and for each answer the question number, the stat, whether it was right and the
    score. Each event carries the country your connection comes from (as Cloudflare reports it) and
    a random run number made for that run alone. There's no IP address, no details of your device or
    browser, and nothing that links one run to another or to you. The one exception is the start of
    a Daily Ranked game, which records how many games had already been started from the same
    connection that day: a number, nothing more. These events are kept for three months.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Endless and Daily Ranked, which have a clock, each answer's event also records how long it
    took to reach our server, measured by the server itself. While an Endless run is in play, the
    server keeps a short record of it — the question you're on, each answer and its time — so that
    no question can be answered twice. That record carries the run number and your country, nothing
    else, and is deleted a few hours after the run ends.
  </p>

  <h2>Daily Ranked</h2>
  <p>
    Daily Ranked is one game a day, played once, and every score goes on that day's{" "}
    <a href={DAILY_LEADERBOARD_PATH}>leaderboard</a>. Your name is reserved when you press Play, so
    nobody else can take it that day, and your score is added when you finish, or when you leave a
    run for a few minutes and the server finishes it for you. We store the name, the score, which
    questions you got right, how long your answers took (only to break ties), when you started and
    finished, and, if you leave "Show my country flag" ticked, your country as a two-letter code.
    These are deleted 100 days after the game; a copy of each game's top 50 is kept, so the page can
    name its winner. As in Endless, an offensive name is retired and a score that looks automated
    may be left off the public board.
  </p>
  <p>
    To allow one game per browser each day, we keep a scrambled form of your browser's random number
    with your entry, and for a few days a record that it has played that day's game. While your run
    is in play, the server keeps a short record of it — the question you're on, each answer and its
    time — deleted a few hours after the run ends.
  </p>
  <p>
    To see how many games are played from the same connection, the server keeps a scrambled form of
    your IP address for each Daily game you start, mixed with a secret key and the game's number, so
    it can't be turned back into the address or matched from one day to the next. It is used only to
    count, never to change or hide a score, and it is deleted after 48 hours.
  </p>

  <h2>The Endless leaderboard</h2>
  <p>
    Publishing a run is opt-in: nothing reaches the leaderboard unless you press Publish at the end
    of a run. When you do, we store:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the nickname you chose (a made-up one is suggested, and you can keep it);</li>
    <li>
      the run's score, the day it started and how long your answers took in total, as our server
      measured them (only to break ties);
    </li>
    <li>
      a scrambled form of the random number your browser keeps. We can't turn it back into the
      number, and it isn't tied to you or your device in any other way: its only job is to keep one
      entry per browser on each board. Clear your browser's data and it's gone.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your nickname and score appear on the public boards for today, this week and this month. We
    don't ask for an email address or any other details, and there's no account. Scores are deleted
    100 days after the day their run started; a copy of each day's, week's and month's top 50 is
    kept, so the page can name each period's winner. A nickname we find offensive is retired: the
    score stays, shown as "Retired name". A score that looks automated may be left off the public
    boards.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you leave "Show my country flag" ticked when you publish, the country your connection comes
    from (as Cloudflare sees it) is kept with the score and shown as a small flag beside your name.
    Only the country is kept, as a two-letter code; never your city, region or address. Untick it
    and no country is kept. Your choice is remembered in your browser for next time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare Turnstile (below) checks each publish too, and the server's usual limit on how fast a
    connection can send applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Twitch Mode</h2>
  <p>
    In <a href={TWITCH_PATH}>Twitch Mode</a> your browser connects straight to Twitch's public chat
    server, read only, as an anonymous guest: there's no Twitch login, no token and no app, and we
    never post in your chat. Chat messages go from Twitch to your browser and nowhere else. They
    never reach our server.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your browser looks at each message only to see whether it's a vote (<code>!h</code>,
    <code>!higher</code>, <code>!l</code>, <code>!lower</code> and so on). It keeps one vote per
    viewer, by their Twitch user number, for the question on screen only, in memory, and forgets
    them all when the next question starts. No message, viewer name or user number is stored, logged
    or sent anywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    What reaches our server with your answer to each question is a count: how many voted and which
    way chat went (higher, lower, a tie or no votes). Our anonymous game statistics keep that, the
    match's settings and both scores, with no names and nothing from the chat itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your browser remembers your channel name, the questions, the number of questions and the voting
    time you last chose, under <code>bt:stream:</code> in local storage, so starting again takes one
    click. Clearing this site's data removes them.
  </p>

  <h2>Server logs</h2>
  <p>
    For three days, our server logs keep a short record of what happened: the start and end of each
    run (with the country, the score and the question that ended it), each run published to the
    leaderboard (its score and rank, never the nickname), requests the server refused, and errors.
    Cloudflare can also log every request automatically, with its IP address and browser details;
    we've switched that off.
  </p>

  <h2>Feedback forms</h2>
  <p>
    The forms, <a href={`${FRIENDLY_PATH}#suggest`}>Suggest a legend</a>,{" "}
    <a href={`${FRIENDLY_PATH}#problem`}>Report a problem</a> and the report on a card at the end of
    a run, send us what you type: the legend's name and your note, your note and the page you were
    on, or your note and the two players, the stat and the figures on that card. None of the forms
    asks for your email address or any other details, and we'd ask you not to put any in.
  </p>
  <p>
    What you send is emailed to us, and we keep the email for as long as we need it to act on what
    you've told us. A copy is kept in our server logs for three days, with your country and nothing
    else about you.
  </p>

  <h3>Cloudflare Turnstile</h3>
  <p>
    Before a form sends, when you start an Endless run or a Daily Ranked game, and when you publish
    a run, Cloudflare Turnstile checks that it's a person and not a bot. It loads when you open a
    form, or on the Endless, Instagram Endless and Daily Ranked pages once the page has loaded —
    never while you're playing Friendly. Most of the time you won't see it. To make that check,
    Cloudflare processes your IP address, details of your connection and your browser's user agent,
    to detect and block bots, under the{" "}
    <a href={TURNSTILE_ADDENDUM}>Cloudflare Turnstile Privacy Addendum</a>. We receive only whether
    the check passed.
  </p>

  <h2>Visit counts</h2>
  <p>
    We use Cloudflare Web Analytics to count visits: how many people view each page, where they
    arrived from, and their country and type of device, all in total. It doesn't use cookies or
    anything else stored in your browser, and it doesn't follow you across sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs the site</h2>
  <p>
    Cloudflare hosts the site and runs its server, and serves the player photos from our own image
    address. The fonts are served by the site itself, so pages don't contact any font service. The
    link to GitHub in the footer takes you to GitHub, which has its own privacy policy.
  </p>

  <h2>No advertising or tracking</h2>
  <p>
    There are no adverts, no advertising or social media trackers, and no data sold or passed to
    anyone for marketing.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this page, or about anything you've sent us? Email us at{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${CORRECTIONS_EMAIL}`}>{CORRECTIONS_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
